BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the syntax of 'Unless', 'Provided that', 'As long as', and 'In case' in biotechnology SOPs.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Conditional Conjunction Taxonomy:) Tj
T*
(   - Unless: Means 'except if' or 'if not'. Introduces negative conditional dependencies:) Tj
T*
(   - "Unless the cell density reaches 80% confluence, do not introduce the transfection vector.") Tj
T*
(   - Caution: Never introduce a second negative word into an 'unless'-clause \(*Unless you do not) Tj
T*
(   add... produces a disastrous double negative\).) Tj
T*
(   - Provided that / Providing that: Emphasizes a strict contractual prerequisite:) Tj
T*
(   - "The recombinant enzyme remains stable at room temperature, provided that the pH is) Tj
T*
(   maintained at 7.4.") Tj
T*
(   - As long as / So long as: Expresses temporal and conditional continuity:) Tj
T*
(   - "As long as dissolved oxygen levels stay above 30%, aerobic respiration continues) Tj
T*
(   uninterrupted.") Tj
T*
(   - In case: Prepares for a prospective contingency \(contrasts with 'if'\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Prepare an extra aliquot of master mix in case pipetting error occurs." \(Precaution taken) Tj
T*
(   regardless of whether the event happens\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
