(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the syntax of 'wish' and 'if only' constructions in speculative scientific literature.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Syntactic Architecture of 'Wish' and 'If Only':) Tj
T*
(   Constructions introduced by 'wish' or 'if only' express regret, hypothetical desire, or) Tj
T*
(   counterfactual dissatisfaction with current empirical states:) Tj
T*
(   - Unreal Present Desire: Takes the Past Subjunctive \(were / past simple\):) Tj
T*
(   - "We wish the crystal resolution were higher than 2.8 Angstroms.") Tj
T*
(   - "If only the enzyme exhibited greater thermal stability at 90 deg C.") Tj
T*
(   - Unreal Past Regret: Takes the Past Perfect:) Tj
T*
(   - "The authors wish they had incorporated a secondary vehicle control in the initial trial.") Tj
T*
(   - "If only the cryo-EM grid had not suffered ice contamination.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Register and Appropriateness:) Tj
T*
(   While 'wish' is common in scientific commentaries, essays, and retrospective interviews, formal) Tj
T*
(   empirical manuscripts replace personal desires with objective modal qualifications \(e.g.,) Tj
T*
(   "Higher resolution would be desirable to resolve side-chain conformers"\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
